{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implemented the narrow runner-binding repair in tests/_isolation.py only: capture subprocess.run once as _run_process at module import, and route both head() and porcelain() through that bound callable. This prevents later monkeypatches of the shared subprocess module from contaminating autouse teardown while leaving the existing 5-second timeout and exception branches unchanged. Scratch proof: both probes ignored a post-import subprocess.run test double; actual blocking Git shims raised TimeoutExpired at 5.00s for both head and porcelain; non-repo, OSError, and CalledProcessError paths still returned None. File-scoped ruff check, ruff format --check, and mypy passed; git diff --check passed. A guessed tests/unit/test_isolation.py invocation was not a test failure—the path does not exist. Held-out oracle files were not read, edited, or run. No commit or push."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787078466193922001,"uuid":"050b1494-8e93-4af6-b3bb-833af2350123"}